.Dd January 14, 2025
.Dt ILSTR 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ilstr_append_char ,
.Nm ilstr_append_str ,
.Nm ilstr_aprintf ,
.Nm ilstr_cstr ,
.Nm ilstr_errno ,
.Nm ilstr_errstr ,
.Nm ilstr_fini ,
.Nm ilstr_init ,
.Nm ilstr_init_prealloc ,
.Nm ilstr_len ,
.Nm ilstr_reset ,
.Nm ilstr_vaprintf
.Nd indeterminate length string builder
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/ilstr.h
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_append_char
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fa "char c"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_append_str
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fa "const char *s"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_aprintf
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fa "const char *fmt"
.Fa ...
.Fc
.Ft "const char *"
.Fo ilstr_cstr
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fc
.Ft ilstr_errno_t
.Fo ilstr_errno
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fc
.Ft "const char *"
.Fo ilstr_errstr
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_fini
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_init
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fa "int kmflag"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_init_prealloc
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fa "char *buf"
.Fa "size_t buflen"
.Fc
.Ft size_t
.Fo ilstr_len
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_reset
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ilstr_vaprintf
.Fa "ilstr_t *ils"
.Fa "const char *fmt"
.Fa "va_list ap"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
.Sy Volatile
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Fa
.It Fa ilstr_t *ils
The indeterminate length string being operated on.
Should be considered opaque by callers.
.It Fa char c
A character to be appended to this string
.It Fa const char *s
A NUL-terminated string to be appended to this string
.It Fa const char *fmt
A
.Xr cmn_err 9f
style format string.
.It Fa int kmflag
Flags as passed to
.Xr kmem_alloc 9f .
Most specifically:
.Bl -tag -width Dv
.It Dv KM_SLEEP
The default, memory allocations may sleep and will never fail (though they may
sleep forever).
.It Dv KM_NOSLEEP
Allocations which would sleep should instead return failure
.El
.It Fa char *buf
A character buffer to be used internally by
.Nm ilstr
in place of allocating its own.
.It Fa size_t buflen
The length of the character buffer provided in
.Fa buf .
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm ilstr
interfaces provide a builder to build and operation on
indeterminate length strings more safely and ergonomically than via the
.Xr string 9f
interfaces.
.Pp
Callers create and manipulate opaque
.Vt ilstr_t
objects which (except
.Fn ilstr_init_prealloc )
grow to arbitrary length as necessary to contain the string
being constructed.
.Pp
.Vt ilstr_t
strings are conceptually never NULL, only empty.
.Ss Initialization
.Fn ilstr_init
and
.Fn ilstr_init_prealloc
each create an
.Vt ilstr_t
(which may be on the stack).
The difference between them is that
.Fn ilstr_init
allocates memory from the heap as necessary to contain the string being built,
whereas
.Fn ilstr_init_prealloc
uses a pre-existing fixed-sized buffer in which to build the string (which may
fail due to
.Dv ILSTR_ERROR_OVERFLOW ) .
This can be useful when building, for example, error messages in fixed size
stack buffers.
.Pp
The initial string is empty and of 0 length.
.Pp
The
.Fn ilstr_reset
function resets an existing
.Vt ilstr_t
to a newly created state.
This does not free associated memory, only empties the string.
.Ss Construction
The
.Fn ilstr_append_char ,
.Fn ilstr_append_str ,
.Fn ilstr_aprintf ,
and
.Fn ilstr_vaprintf
functions append characters, strings and formatted strings to the
.Vt ilstr_t
respectively.
.Ss Retrieval
.Fn ilstr_cstr
returns the string representation of this
.Vt ilstr_t
as a NUL-terminated "C" string.
This can never be
.Dv NULL
but may be the empty string "".
.Fn ilstr_len
returns the length in bytes of the string this
.Vt ilstr_t
represents.
.Ss Error handling
.Fn ilstr_errno
and
.Fn ilstr_errstr
provide access to the error number and a descriptive error string
respectively.
.Fn ilstr_errno
should be checked to determine the success of prior string building
operations, prior to making use of the string.
.Ss Disposal
.Fn ilstr_fini
should be called to dispose of the
.Vt ilstr_t .
This will free any associated memory, and fully dispose of the string.
.Sh CONTEXT
These interfaces may always be used from
.Sy user
or
.Sy kernel
context.
Additionally,
.Vt ilstr_t
initialized using
.Fn ilstr_init_prealloc
or with
.Dv KM_NOSLEEP
may be manipulated in
.Sy interrupt
context.
.Vt ilstr_t
initialized with
.Dv KM_NOSLEEP
may be used during early boot prior to
.Xr kmem_alloc 9f
being available.
.Sh ERRORS
Errors should be checked using the
.Fn ilstr_errno
interface, which returns an
.Vt ilstr_errno_t .
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er ILSTR_ERROR_OK
No errors have occurred
.It Er ILSTR_ERROR_NOMEM
The operation on this
.Vt ilstr_t
requires more memory, allocation failed, and this
.Vt ilstr_t
was allocated using
.Dv KM_NOSLEEP .
.It Er ILSTR_ERROR_OVERFLOW
The string being constructed has a length not representable by
.Vt size_t .
.It Er ILSTR_ERROR_PRINTF
.Fn ilstr_aprintf
or
.Fn ilstr_vaprintf
received invalid format arguments.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kmem_alloc 9f ,
.Xr string 9f
